1 OCCU prins van Oranje, Fürst von Oranien Grfaf von Nassau-Diillenburg, Erbstatthalter der Niederlande (1625 - 1650)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederik_Hendrik_of_Orange
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00000097&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Henry, or Frederik Hendrik in Dutch (29 January 1584 – 14 March 1647), was the sovereign Prince of Orange and stadtholder of Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Guelders, and Overijssel from 1625 to 1647.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Henry was born on 29 January 1584 in Delft, Holland, Dutch Republic. He was the youngest child of William the Silent and Louise de Coligny. His father William was stadtholder of Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, and Friesland. 
2 CONC His mother Louise was daughter of the Huguenot leader Gaspard de Coligny, and was the fourth wife of his father. He was thus the half brother of his predecessor Maurice of Orange, deceased in 1625.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Henry was born six months before his father's assassination on 10 July 1584. The boy was trained to arms by his elder brother Maurice, one of the finest generals of his age. After Maurice threatened to legimitize his ill
2 CONC egitimate children if he did not marry, Frederick Henry married Amalia of Solms-Braunfels in 1625. His illegitimate son by Margaretha Catharina Bruyns (1595–1625), Frederick Nassau de Zuylenstein was born in 1624 before his marria
2 CONC ge. This son later became the governor of the young William III of England for seven years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stadtholder
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Maurice in 1625 without legitimate issue, Frederick Henry succeeded him in his paternal dignities and estates, and also in the stadtholderates of the five provinces of Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Overijssel and Guel
2 CONC ders, and in the important posts of captain and admiral-general of the Union (commander-in-chief of the Dutch States Army and of the Dutch navy).
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Henry proved himself almost as good a general as his brother, and a far more capable statesman and politician. For twenty-two years he remained at the head of government in the United Provinces, and in his time the power
2 CONC  of the stadtholderate reached its highest point. The "Period of Frederick Henry," as it is usually styled by Dutch writers, is generally accounted the golden age of the republic. It was marked by great military and naval triumphs
2 CONC , by worldwide maritime and commercial expansion, and by a wonderful outburst of activity in the domains of art and literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT The chief military exploits of Frederick Henry were the sieges and captures of Grol in 1627, 's-Hertogenbosch in 1629, of Maastricht in 1632, of Breda in 1637, of Sas van Gent in 1644, and of Hulst in 1645. During the greater part
2 CONC  of his administration the alliance with France against Spain had been the pivot of Frederick Henry's foreign policy, but in his last years he sacrificed the French alliance for the sake of concluding a separate peace with Spain, 
2 CONC by which the United Provinces obtained from that power all the advantages they had been seeking for eighty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Henry built the country houses Huis Honselaarsdijk, Huis ter Nieuwburg, and for his wife Huis ten Bosch, and he renovated the Noordeinde Palace in The Hague. Huis Honselaarsdijk and Huis ter Nieuwburg are now demolished.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Henry died on 14 March 1647 in The Hague, Holland, Dutch Republic. He left a wife, a son William II, Prince of Orange, four daughters, and the illegitimate son Frederick Nassau de Zuylenstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Frederick Henry's death, he was buried with great pomp beside his father and brother at Delft. The treaty of Munster, ending the long struggle between the Dutch and the Spaniards, was not actually signed until 30 January 1648, 
2 CONC the illness and death of the stadtholder having caused a delay in the negotiations. Frederick Henry left an account of his campaigns in his Mémoires de Frédéric Henri (Amsterdam, 1743). See Cambridge Mod. Hist. vol. iv. chap. 24.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Henry and his wife Amalia van Solms had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  William II, Prince of Orange (27 May 1626 – 6 November 1650)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Luise Henriette of Nassau (27 December 1627 – 18 June 1667)
