1 NAME Marguerite /Robin/
2 GIVN Marguerite
2 SURN Robin
2 _MARNM Robin
1 SEX F
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://jean.gallian.free.fr/comm2/b/bon-meuilhon.htm 
2 CONT Google translation from French.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bon , Baron of Mévouillon n:
2 CONT Source: Directory of the works of the Marseille Statistical Society Society p.126
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''André Bon''' married Jeanne de Tanne.  Their son was:
2 CONT **'''Pierre Bon''', Baron of Mévouillon (alias Meuillon, Mévillon, Méolhon, Mévolhon) and Montauban, gentleman of the King's household (1), captain of two galleys, governor of Notre-Dame de la Garde (2), governor of Marseille (156
2 CONC 2), knight of Saint-Michel (1565), State Councilor, lieutenant general for the King in Provence, .... son of André Bon and Jeanne de Tanne; married around 1540 with '''Marguerite de Robin,''' daughter of Etienne II, Lord of Graves
2 CONC on and partly of Barbentane, ...Pierre Bon died in Marseille on January 10, 1578 and was buried, according to his wishes, in the chapel of Noire-Dame de la Garde. He left:
2 CONT ***1° '''Claude-Antoine Bon, Baron of Mévouillon and Montauban''', governor of Notre-Dame de la Garde (1575), Grand Chamberlain of the Duke of Savoy, married, on November 30, 1600. with '''Béatrix de Coligny''', Countess of Montbe
2 CONC l and d'Entremont, Marquise of Monteiller and Saint-André-de-Briord, lady-in-waiting to Catherine, Infanta of Spain, Duchess of Savoy; posthumous daughter of Gaspard II, Count of Coligny, Lord of Châtillon, Colonel General of the 
2 CONC Infantry (1547), Admiral of France (1551), etc., assassinated on St. Bartholomew's Day (August 24, 1572)  and of his second wife, Jacqueline de Montbel, Countess of Montbel and d'Entremont, widow of Claude de Bastarnay, Count of B
2 CONC ouchage, killed at the Battle of Saint-Denis, November 10, 1567 (7).
2 CONT ****  The remuneration of''' the children of Claude-Antoine Bon, who took the name and arms of Montbel,''' can be found in the History of Bresse and Bugey (3rd part, p. 171), by Guichenon. 
2 CONT ***  2°''' Hélène Bon''', married in first marriage to Charles de Gondi (1536-1574), lord of La Tour, knight of Saint-Michel, ... and in second marriage to Charles de Balzac , lord of Clermont-Soubiran, knight of the Holy Spirit (
2 CONC 1583), ... from where ... 
2 CONT ****from where: Louise and Marie de Balzac, daughters of Henri, son of the aforementioned Charles, marquis of Clermont d'Entragues, count of Graville, baron of Dunes, and of Louise L'Huillier.
2 CONT *****Louise, who died in March 1682, married, on September 3, 1647, Louis de Bretagne-Avaugour, Marquis d'Avaugour, Count of Vertus and Goëllo, whose first wife she was; 
2 CONT *****Marie, who died on November 9, 1691, married, on May 28, 1651, Jean-Gaspard Ferdinand, Count of Marchin and of the Holy Empire, Knight of the Order of the Garter, Captain and General Camp Master of the King of Spain in the Ne
2 CONC therlands (11). Mmes de Balzac were therefore granddaughters of Hélène Bon, great-granddaughters of Pierre Bon and great-nieces of Claude-Antoine Bon.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NOTE on Mévouillon:
2 CONT
2 CONT From the feudal point of view; Mévouillon was a land of the ancient barony of that name, which, at first sovereignly possessed by the Mévouillon, whom the emperor Frederick I endowed in 1178 with all the sovereign rights in their 
2 CONC domains, and who gave in 1270 a charter of liberties to their vassals, was homage in 1230 to the bishops of Die and given in 1293 to the Dauphins. Thus having become state land, Mévouillon was sold with the option of repurchase to
2 CONC  the Louet or Lovat, in 1421, to the Bon in 1553 , to the Calignon in 1573, finally to the La Tour-Gouvernet in 1593. Passed by marriage to the Du Puy-Montbrun in 1638, it returned in 1711 to the La Tour-Gouvernet, who were lords 
2 CONC of it until the Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT http://jean.gallian.free.fr/comm2/b/bon-meuilhon.htm cites
2 CONT (1) by letters given at Saint-Germain-en-Laye on September 7, 1538 (Arch. des B.-du-R., B, 36, fol. 75).
2 CONT (2) by letters given at Hyères on November 5, 1540 (Arch. des B.-du-R., B; 34, fol. 265 v°).
2 CONT (3) ART, t. II, p. 329.
2 CONT (4) letters granted to the first at Amboise, in April 1540, and to the second at Fontainebleau, on November 19 of the same year (Arch. des B.-du-R., B, 35, fol. 14 and 11).
2 CONT (5) Arch. des B.-du-R., B, 38, fol. 72.
2 CONT (6) Arch. des B.-du-R, B, 42, fol. 12 v°
2 CONT (7) P. ANSELME, op. cit., t. VII, p. 153.
2 CONT (8) RUFFI, op. cit., p. 395.
2 CONT (9) Arch. of the B.-du-R., seneschalcy of Marseille, 1620-1624, fol. 884.
2 CONT (10) P. ANSELME, op. cit., t. II, p. 894.
2 CONT (11) P. ANSELME, op. cit. t. II, p, . 440.
