1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT From Wikipedia  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois_de_la_Noue
2 CONT
2 CONT ==François de La Noue==
2 CONT
2 CONT François de la Noue (1531 – August 14, 1591), called Bras-de-Fer (Iron Arm), was one of the Huguenot captains of the 16th century. He was born near Nantes in 1531, of an ancient Breton family.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in Italy under Marshal Brissac, and in the first Huguenot war, but his first great exploit was the capture of Orléans at the head of only fifteen cavaliers in 1567, during the second war. During the third war, at the bat
2 CONC tle of Jarnac in March 1569 he commanded the rearguard, and at Moncontour the following October he was taken prisoner; but he was exchanged in time to resume the governorship of Poitou, and to inflict a signal defeat on the royali
2 CONC st troops before Rochefort.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the siege of Fontenay (1570) his left arm was shattered by a bullet and later amputated; but a mechanic of La Rochelle made him an artificial iron arm (hence his sobriquet) with a hook for holding his reins. When peace was made
2 CONC  in France in the same year, La Noue carried his sword against the Spaniards in the Netherlands, but was taken at the recapture of Mons by the Spanish in 1572.
2 CONT
2 CONT Permitted to return to France, he was commissioned by Charles IX, after the St. Bartholomew's Day massacre, to reconcile the inhabitants of La Rochelle, the great stronghold of the Huguenots, to the king (see Siege of La Rochelle 
2 CONC (1572-1573)). But the Rochellois were too much alarmed to come to terms; and La Noue, perceiving that war was imminent, and knowing that his post was on the Huguenot side, gave up his royal commission, and from 1574 till 1578 acte
2 CONC d as general of La Rochelle.
2 CONT
2 CONT When peace was again concluded La Noue once more went to aid the Protestants of the Low Countries. In 1579, together with the Englishman John Norreys, he led the Dutch States' army at the Battle of Borgerhout, where Alexander Farn
2 CONC ese, Spanish Governor of the Netherlands, defeated them. He took several towns and captured Count Egmont in 1580; but a few weeks afterwards he fell into the hands of the Spaniards. Thrust into a prison at Limburg, La Noue was kep
2 CONC t confined for five years.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was in captivity that he wrote his celebrated Discours politiques et militaires, a work which was then published at Basel in 1587, La Rochelle in 1590, London (in English) in 1587, Frankfurt on Main (in German) 1592 and 1612 an
2 CONC d had an immense influence on the soldiers of all nations. The abiding value of La Noue's Discourses lies in the fact that he wrote of war as a human drama, before it had been elaborated and codified.
2 CONT
2 CONT At length, in June 1585, La Noue was exchanged for Egmont and other important prisoners, while a heavy ransom and a pledge not to bear arms against the King of Spain were also exacted from him. Between 1586 and 1589 La Noue lived 
2 CONC in Geneve and took no part in public matters, but in that year he joined Henry of Navarre against the Leaguers. He was present at both sieges of Paris, at Ivry and other battles. At the siege of Lamballe in Brittany he received a 
2 CONC wound of which he died at Moncontour on August 4, 1591.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Works===
2 CONT
2 CONT He wrote, besides the Discourses,
2 CONT
2 CONT * Declaration pour prise d'armées et la défeute de Sedan et Jarnets (1588)
2 CONT * Observations sur l'histoire de Guicciardini, 2 vols, (1592)
2 CONT * notes on Plutarch's Lives.
2 CONT * His Correspondence was published in 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT * La Vie de François, seigneur de La Noue, by Moyse Amirault (Leiden, 1661)
2 CONT * Pierre de Brantôme, Vies des Capitaines français
2 CONT * C. Vincens, François de La Noue, dit Bras de Fer (1875)
2 CONT * Henri Hauser, François de La Noue (Paris, 1892).
2 CONT *  This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Chisholm, Hugh, ed. (1911). "La Noue, François de". Encyclopædia Britannica. 16 (11th ed.). Cambridge University Press. pp. 183–184.
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT * Works by or about François de la Noue at Internet Archive
2 CONT * Works by or about Bras-de-Fer at Internet Archive
