1 OCCU Count of Laval, Comte de Laval, vicomte de Rennes, baron de Vitré, de La Roche-Bernard, d´Acquigny, de Quintin, du Perrier etc.gouverneur et lieutenant-général en Bretagne, capitaine de Rennes, amiral de Bretagne.
1 TITL Vicomte de Rennes, Count de Laval
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Guy XVI de MONTMORENCY-LAVAL
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas de MONTMORENCY-LAVAL comte de Montfort et de Laval, baron de Quintin, seigneur de Vitré, de Gavre, de La Roche-Bernard et d'Acquigny, amiral de Bretagne, sgr de Boisorcant
2 CONT
2 CONT http://gw.geneanet.org/pierfit?lang=fr;p=nicolas;n=de+montmorency+laval
2 CONT
2 CONT from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Guy XVI, Count of Laval''', Mayenne (1 October 1476 – 20 May 1531) was a member of the House of Laval. He was christened '''Nicolas''', but upon inheriting the title, he took the required name of Guy, as his predecessors had do
2 CONC ne. He was the son of Jean de Laval, who was the brother of Guy XV and the son of Guy XIV and Isabella of Brittany. Descended from royalty, he was the ancestor of kings of France and Belgium through his daughter Anne, and ancestor
2 CONC  of William III of England through his daughter Charlotte.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Career and Court
2 CONT * 2 Marriages and Family
2 CONT
2 CONT =Career and Court=
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born Nicolas de Laval, lord of La Roche-Bernard. He spent much of his youth at the court of his kinsman, Francis II, Duke of Brittany, where he became close with the Duke's daughter, Anne. When Francis died and Anne married
2 CONC  Charles VIII of France, Nicolas followed her to the royal court, where they remained close. The queen became godmother to his daughter Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy XVI served the Kings of France with distinction throughout his career. This included service as the Lieutenant General (from 1517), Governor (from 1525), and Admiral of Brittany, where many of his lands were. Guy XVI frequentl
2 CONC y corresponded with his brother-in-law, Anne de Montmorency to gain insight and favors at the royal court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy XVI maintained a large court of lesser nobles from throughout France and beyond. His lands included Vitré, Lohéac, Montfort, Becherel, Quintin, and La Roche-Bernard. In 1527 he had 162 people being directly paid within his hou
2 CONC sehold, and biographers estimated that there were on average 300 people at his court on any given day. For a time, he hosted a representative of the King of Portugal, who came with gifts for Guy. Guy was a strong patron of the art
2 CONC s, and also enjoyed jousting. At the tournament celebrating the arrival of Mary Tudor in Paris, he jousted over 40 times.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriages and Family=
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy XVI first married Charlotte of Naples on 10 Jun 1500. This marriage was arranged by his cousin, Anne of Brittany. Their children included:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Catherine, who married Claude I de Rieux. Their daughter became Guyonne, Countess of Laval from 1547 to 1567.
2 CONT * François (d 1522 at the Battle of Bicocca)
2 CONT * Anne de Laval (1505-1554), who married François II de La Trémoille
2 CONT * His first wife died in 1506. In 1517, he married Anne de Montmorency, sister of the Constable of France with the same name. Their children included:
2 CONT
2 CONT          * Claude, who inherited the title as Guy XVII (r 1531-1547)
2 CONT          * Marguerite, who married Louis V of Rohan
2 CONT          * Anne de Laval (Acquigny) (fr), who married Louis de Silly
2 CONT
2 CONT His second wife died in 1525. Some time after that, he married Antoinette de Daillon. They had at least one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Charlotte, who married Gaspard II de Coligny
