1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andreas_von_Auersperg
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas von Auersperg, Lord of Schönberg und Seisenberg (Slovene: Andrej Turjaški; Croatian and Bosnian: Andrija Auersperg) (9 April 1556 – 5 September 1593) was a Carniolan noble and leader of the defending forces at the Battle o
2 CONC f Sisak in 1593.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life and Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas von Auersperg was born in Carniolan Seisenberg into one of the leading Protestant Austrian families in the Duchy of Carniola as the youngest son of Wolfgang-Engelbert von Auersperg, Lord of Schönberg, Seisenberg and Flödni
2 CONC g, and Anna Maria von Lamberg. After his parents' early demise the governor of Carniola, Baron Weikhard von Auersperg (1533–1581), became the guardian of the one-year-old boy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1569 the 13-year-old registered at the University of Tübingen, where the Collegiate Church, along with the rest of the city, was one of the first to have converted to Martin Luther's teaching, but also, in 1573 and 1574, studie
2 CONC d at the renowned universities of Padua and Bologna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas became a soldier accompanying Archduke Matthew on his campaign in the in die Netherlands 1577–1578, fighting as a captain on the Croatian-Turkish border in 1578 and 1579 under Hans Ferenberger von Auer and Christoph von Au
2 CONC ersperg. 1583 he rose to the rank of colonel and was appointed commander-in-chief ("Feldobrist") of the Croatian and Dalmatian frontier lands in Karlstadt in 1589.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 22, 1593, the day of Saint Acacius,[3] the leader of the ten thousand martyrs, a battle occurred near the fortress of Sisak in present day Croatia where the Sava and Kupa rivers meet. It was the last fortress the Turks nee
2 CONC ded to conquer in order to expand northward into central Europe virtually unopposed. Sources report[4] that the Turkish Army attacking the fortress was 38,000 strong, commanded by the Bosnian Beylerbey (Governor-General), Hasan Pa
2 CONC sha (who was born as "Niko Predojević“). The Carniolan army under the command of the Ban of Croatia, Tamás Erdődy, which was defending the fort counted only 4,000 to 5,000 men led by Andreas von Auersperg and Ruprecht von Eggenber
2 CONC g and reinforced by 1,240 Croatian horsemen and 500 Silesian mounted riflemen. Hasan Predojević attacked with his main force but was repelled by the heavy fire of the defending army. The Turks then retreated to the bridge they had
2 CONC  just crossed, but Auersperg sent the Arquebusars to capture the bridge. The Turks were then forced to swim to the other side of the river. About 8,000 Turks died during the retreat, including Hasan Pasha, who drowned in the river
2 CONC . The remaining Turks (who were guarding the camp) set their gunpowder on fire and fled. Thus, Auersperg/Turjaški won the Battle of Sisak and saved central Europe from imminent Turkish invasion, whereupon Pope Clement VIII sent th
2 CONC e Protestant, who was nicknamed the "Carniolan Achilles" or even the "Christian Achill(es)" and called "the Terror of the Turks“, a handwritten letter of congratulation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas von Auersperg died unmarried in Karlovac three months later.
