1 TITL (Gen 18)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jeanne d’Halluin, famously known as "La Petite Pienne," was the "Protagonist of the Secret Vow" whose private heart became a matter of French national security. While her Flemish cousins secured the Spanish border,
2 CONC  Jeanne’s clandestine betrothal to the heir of the House of Montmorency triggered a royal crisis that reached the Pope and fundamentally altered French marriage laws. Her eventual marriage to Florimond III Robertet was not merely 
2 CONC a union of nobility, but a calculated "strategic retreat" that anchored the Pienne branch within the highest echelons of the Valois administrative machine.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''(Gen 16) Jeanne d’Halluin, Dame d'Alluye; Dame de Maignelay'''
2 CONT
2 CONT “The Protagonist of the Secret Vow and Medicean Envoy”
2 CONT
2 CONT Active Period: c. 1545 – 1581.
2 CONT Status: Fille d’honneur to Catherine de’ Medici; High-Noble of the French Court.
2 CONT Strategic Role: The Pienne Representative at Court. Known as "La Petite Pienne," Jeanne was a pivotal figure in the family's "Double-Lock" strategy—maintaining deep influence within the French inner circle while her Flemish cousin
2 CONC s managed the Spanish Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. Genealogical Data Points (Audited)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: (Gen 15) Anthonis van Halewijn (Antoine d'Halluin), Seigneur de Piennes and Maignelay.
2 CONT Mother: (Gen 15) Louise de Crèvecoeur, Dame de Crèvecoeur.
2 CONT Spouse (Official): Florimond III Robertet, Seigneur d’Alluye.
2 CONT Betrothed (Contested): François, duc de Montmorency.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. The Montmorency Scandal: A Crisis of State'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne’s life was defined by one of the most famous legal scandals of the 16th-century French court.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Secret Vow: While serving as a maid of honor, Jeanne and François de Montmorency (heir to the Constable of France) exchanged a secret promise of marriage (verba de futuro).
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal Intervention: The union was viewed as a threat to royal plans for François to marry the King's daughter, Diane de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Forced Separation: Under intense pressure from King Henry II, Jeanne was imprisoned in the convent of Filles-Dieu. The betrothal was forcibly annulled through controversial ecclesiastical maneuvers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legislative Legacy: This specific Halluin scandal directly led to the Edict of 1556 against Clandestine Marriages, which fundamentally altered French law by requiring parental consent for marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Marriage to Florimond III Robertet'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the fallout with the House of Montmorency, a strategic marriage was rapidly arranged to stabilize the family's standing.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Robertet Alliance: In 1559, she married Florimond III Robertet d’Alluye, a Secretary of State.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Result: This marriage served as a "strategic retreat". It provided her with the title Dame d'Alluye and successfully re-established the Pienne branch’s favor at court following the royal friction of the previous years.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary Arms: Argent, three lions rampant sable, armed, langued, and crowned or.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Alliance Shield: Her personal seals displayed the Halluin Lions impaled with the Escars or Robertet arms, reflecting the "French" shift of the Maignelay-Piennes branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Visual Note: As a dame of the French provinces, she likely favored the ornate Renaissance styles of the Valois court over the rigid Spanish sobriety of her Flemish cousins.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. Curator Logic: The "Political Pivot"'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [!IMPORTANT]
2 CONT Forensic Conclusion: Jeanne represents "High-Stakes Diplomacy."
2 CONT
2 CONT Curator Logic identifies Jeanne as a figure who nearly vaulted the Halluin family to the absolute pinnacle of the French peerage via the Montmorency betrothal. While the marriage failed, her subsequent union with Robertet ensured 
2 CONC that the "Pienne" branch remained an essential, landed anchor in the French royal administration.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. Primary Sources (The "Auditors" Trail)'''
2 CONT * "Histoire de la Maison de Montmorency": Details the legal battle over the secret vow.
2 CONT * Letters of Catherine de' Medici: References to her maid of honor "La Petite Pienne" during the crisis.
2 CONT * Edict of 1556 (Henry II): The primary legal document born from the Jeanne d'Halluin scandal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ⚠️ CAUTION: NAMESAKE COLLISION WARNING ⚠️
2 CONT
2 CONT Do not confuse this (Gen 16) Jeanne d'Halluin with earlier or later generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT THIS PROFILE: Daughter of Anthonis/Antoine; the woman whose secret vow changed French law.
2 CONT
2 CONT DO NOT CONFUSE WITH: Jeanne d'Halluin (Gen 14) who married into the House of Crèvecoeur.
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">This Biographical summary is drafted with AI assistance, cross-referenced with the House of Halluin and Montmorency historical records. (This summary is intended for research leads only; 
2 CONC cross-verification with original primary documentation is essential for final lineage certification.)</span>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Voir [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeanne_d'Halluin '''Wikipedia...''']
