1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Antionette D'DUMOND was born about 1555 in Neuvy-sur-Loire, Nievre, France. From the Maybee Society files. The data is not verified and is possibly incorrect.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note: it is unclear why Antoinette's family name would be D'DUMOND since the du part is already de + le. D'Dumond would read, “of of the Mount.” It would be interesting to peruse the original documents from which the names d'Mail
2 CONC ly and d'Dumond were taken.] This is clearly a spelling error. The name "de Dumond" cannot exist. Nor can " d'Dumond " exist:  d' must precede a vowel. "Du" already serves as the article.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, her father could not have been known as a duke if his parentage was unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unlikely that if Mabille was actually the grandson of a lord, and thus a descendant of the important royal houses of Europe and a close cousin of the Bourbon kings of France, that he would marry someone with unknown parentag
2 CONC e with a concocted title of duke, during the 16th century. It is also unlikely that the grandson of a lord would marry the daughter of a duke. Daughters of Dukes marry Kings, not a man absent a title, even if a grandson of a minor
2 CONC  lord. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, the grandson of a duke would never be a low-ranking sergeant officer.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, note that Gaspard de Mabille is the only child who uses the name with a "B". Mabille versus Mailley. Usually when the spelling changes, the consonants don't, such as: Smyth versus Smith or Smithe. They still sound the same. 
2 CONC Maybee, Mabie and Mabille all sound the same. Mailly doesn't. Mailley would sound as MayLee and Mabille would sound as MayBee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, see that the only descendants give as grandchildren were from the Mabille. Yet, the Mailly son was the actual heir. This pedigree was not given by the Mailly family.
